From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] win32u, user32: a per-monitor aware program's menus, dialogs and icons follow a new display scale

A per-monitor aware program lays itself out at a new display scale
(WM_DPICHANGED, 0890), but what Wine draws for it -- its menus, message
boxes, dialogs, the common controls' sizes, the icons and cursors it loads
-- went by the system DPI it started with: after 100% -> 175% its popup
menus and new dialogs were 100%'s, tiny on the screen, and its menu bar's
items kept 100%'s spacing. On Windows the system DPI also stays, but the
system scales those per monitor; Wine does not. On our one display such a
process's system DPI is now the monitor's: it follows the display scale
(follow_display_scale), so GetDpiForSystem, LOGPIXELSY, the system metrics,
the dialog base units and new menus, dialogs and icons are the new
scale's. A thread or window made aware of the system DPI keeps the DPI it
was made at (its context carries it); a context taken before is put back.
A per-monitor v1 window's frame is laid out at the new DPI too, so its
suggested rectangle includes it. The menu font and the menu's character
size are made per DPI (they were made once per process). A program that is
not per-monitor aware keeps its DPI, and Wine scales it, as before.

At 100% with nothing changing, nothing changes.

Test: test/dpifollow-gate.sh part 2 (mutants SG_MUTANT_SYSDPI_FIXED in
sysparams.c, SG_MUTANT_MENU_FONT_ONCE in menu.c); test/dpilive-gate.sh.
